   ],
   "l": "Thirty spokes hold one hub in common;\nat its nothing —\nthere is the cart's use.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Thirty spokes come together at one hub; and it is where the hub is empty that the cart has its use.",
   "n": "Numbers first: 三十 is 'three-ten', which is how Chinese builds every number from twenty to ninety-nine — one word, two characters. Then the argument. 當其無 is 'at its nothing', and the 無 here is not a metaphysical void but the round hole in the hub that the axle passes through; Wang Bi's comment says plainly that what lets a hub gather thirty spokes is that it is empty. Notice again that nothing supplies an 'it is … that': 有車之用 is simply 'there is | the cart's | use'.",
   "p": true
  },
