   "l": "Knead clay and thereby make a vessel;\nat its nothing —\nthere is the vessel's use.\nCut out door and window and thereby make a chamber;\nat its nothing —\nthere is the chamber's use.\nTherefore that there-is thereby makes the benefit,\nthat there-is-not thereby makes the use.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Knead the clay to make a vessel: it is the hollow inside that gives the vessel its use. Cut out a door and a window to make a room: it is the empty space that gives the room its use. So what is there yields the benefit; what is not there yields the use.",
   "n": "以為 is 以 'by means of (it)' plus 為 'make', with the object left unsaid — 'take [clay] and thereby make a vessel'. You will meet the pair on nearly every page. The closing couplet is the chapter's thesis and its hardest line. The 之 of 有之 / 無之 is a third job for that character, and the one that catches people: it is neither the 'of' of 車之用 (a genitive needs a noun after it, and here what follows is a verb) nor the 'it' of 得之. It stands between a subject and its predicate and welds them into a single noun-clause — 'that-there-is makes for benefit'. Wang Bi construes it exactly so, paraphrasing the line as 有之所以為利, 'the way in which what-is-there makes for benefit', and adding that wood, clay and walls complete these three things only because each of them leans on what is empty in order to be of use. Keep the last two nouns apart: 利 is profit, the good you get out of a thing; 用 is use, what the thing actually does."
  }
